@startuml levenshtein-pathmap-pipeline
' The two distinct ways PathMap enters libgrammstein — they are routinely
' conflated. (A) PathMapDictionary is an IN-MEMORY libdictenstein backend that
' satisfies MutableMappedDictionary, so the trainer and the Levenshtein automaton
' use it like any other trie. (B) pathmap::PathMap is used DIRECTLY by
' PathMapTranslator to turn a trained ARTrie into a compact, memory-mappable
' production artifact. Only (B) touches the `pathmap` crate's own API.
' Labels use Unicode glyphs, not <latex>: house style, see docs/diagrams/README.md.
' Render: plantuml -tsvg docs/diagrams/levenshtein-pathmap-pipeline.puml
' Source of truth for Figure 1 in docs/integration/liblevenshtein/pathmap-synergy.md §3.
skinparam backgroundColor #FAFAFA
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultTextAlignment center
skinparam linetype ortho
skinparam rectangle {
  FontColor white
  FontStyle bold
  BorderColor #263238
  roundCorner 12
}
skinparam ArrowColor #37474F
skinparam ArrowFontColor #37474F
skinparam NoteBackgroundColor #ECEFF1
skinparam NoteBorderColor #90A4AE
skinparam NoteFontColor #263238
title PathMap in libgrammstein — one crate, two entry points

legend top right
  |= Color |= Concept |
  |<#1565C0>     | Statistical LM core (libgrammstein) |
  |<#00838F>     | Storage / persistence |
  |<#546E7A>     | External crate (pathmap · libdictenstein) |
endlegend

package "(A) In-memory backend — reached through libdictenstein" {
  rectangle "**TrainerBuilder**\n""TrainerBuilder::new(dict).order(5)""" as trainer #1565C0
  rectangle "**PathMapDictionary<NgramEntry>**\n""libdictenstein::pathmap""\nimpl MutableMappedDictionary\n+ IterableDictionary" as pmd #546E7A
  rectangle "**PathMapNgramModel**\nNgramModel<PathMapDictionary<NgramEntry>>\nshared subtries ⟹ cheap snapshots\n(no serde: PathMap-native persistence)" as pmm #1565C0
}

package "(B) Production artifact — the pathmap crate directly" {
  rectangle "**PersistentARTrieChar<u64>**\ntrained on disk: WAL + checkpoints\n(the corpus-scale training format)" as artrie #00838F
  rectangle "**PathMapTranslator**\n""src/sources/google_books/translator.rs""\nskips \\x00-prefixed MKN metadata" as tr #1565C0
  rectangle "**PathMap<u64>**\n""pathmap::PathMap""\nradix-256 trie **DAG**\nprefix compression · structural sharing" as pm #546E7A
  rectangle "**paths file**\n""serialize_paths_with_auxdata""\nACT-compressed · merkleized · mmap-able" as paths #00838F
}

trainer --> pmd : writes through\nupdate_or_insert(&self, …)
pmd --> pmm : backs
pmm --> pmd : Transducer queries it\nlike any other trie

artrie --> tr : ""iter_with_values()""
tr --> pm : ""insert(key.as_bytes(), value)""
pm --> paths : serialize
paths --> tr : verify() re-reads via\nfor_each_deserialized_path

note bottom of pmd
  PathMapDictionary is a **trie**, not a hash map: lookup is
  𝒪(m) in the term length m and prefix walks are supported —
  so the Levenshtein **Transducer** can drive it exactly as it
  drives a DAWG. Any doc claiming "𝒪(1) hash, no prefix
  search, no fuzzy matching" is describing something else.
end note

note bottom of paths
  Deployment reads are memory-mapped, so the resident set is the
  **working set**, not the whole model. TranslationStats reports
  entries_translated, the two sizes, and their ratio
  (ARTrie bytes ÷ PathMap bytes).
end note

note right of tr
  CLI:  ""libgrammstein convert to-pathmap""
  ""--input <model> --output <model>.paths""
end note

@enduml
